00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
let's talk about one of the most  secretive countries in the world and  what happens when covet hits  north korea says it's kept the virus out  for the last two years but not anymore  north korea has acknowledged its very  first cases of covert 19. supreme leader  kim jong-un said the country faced great  turmoil and ordered a lockdown of all  cities  no one's vaccinated testing capacity is  seriously limited and the health care  system is weak wha is deeply concerned  at the risk of further spread of covet  19 in the country it's already a  economically impoverished country so  it's just going to make matters worse so  what do we know about what's happening  and did north korea really keep cobit  out all this time  here's a throwback it's january 20th  2020. a new coronavirus has been  identified in china spreading from the  city of wuhan there have been  282 confirmed cases across four  countries and the world is only just  starting to make sense of it all but  north korea takes action even before the  chinese authorities lock down the  province which  is in the north koreans stopped all  travel and sealed their borders  so this is not something new they've  done the same thing for sars mers and  ebola and north korea basically kept  this up until january this year and says  there were zero cases in all that time  but the thing about north korea is that  it's almost impossible to get accurate  information  and there have been various reports of  coveted cases there over the last two  years there were a lot of reports about  fever outbreaks or people getting sick  in a certain city and the residents  didn't really know if it was covered but  they suspected it was coveted what we do  know is that on may 12th the government  admitted it had a problem it announced  it had detected the omicron variant and  it was spreading fast and some north  korea watchers reckon that two big  public celebrations back in april could  have been super spreader events  since the big announcement state media  has been reporting daily figures for  what they're calling a fever by may 18th  they'd hit the 2 million mark and  confirmed 63 deaths but the number of  new cases appeared to have peaked a  couple days later keep in mind though  these figures come with a couple of  caveats so there's a little asterisk  every time we talk about new cases in  north korea right because what they're  reporting are the number of cases with  fever and it speaks directly to the fact  that they completely lack capacity to  monitor and run tests on these cases to  confirm as with anything that's dealing  with north korea and the internal  situation we're basically going by what  the north korean government is is  publicizing to its internal and also  its external audiences now remember  omicron is less deadly than earlier  covert 19 variants but it's super  infectious and north korea is  particularly vulnerable no one has been  vaccinated so it's going to rip through  the population the leadership has  repeatedly refused offers of vaccines  including from kovacs the global vaccine  program their refusal to vaccinate their  population was really based on that  the belief that the prevention would  work and could stay on until the  pandemic is over i don't know if it's as  much of a concern about  their reputation abroad or the  you know being beholden to a foreign  party because of receiving vaccines it  might just be because they don't want to  risk personnel and foreigners coming  into the country going around the  country typical concerns about seeing  the real state of things and then there  are other problems with north korea's  health care system now by some measures  it's not too bad for a low-income  country for example they have plenty of  trained doctors nurses and technicians  but they're dealing with serious  shortages including of basic drugs like  paracetamol  the other thing is the lack of resources  in terms of medical equipment  being able to exchange  information with outside world that's  also severely limited and then on top of  that you have the international  sanctions which also shackles north  korea's  ability to get parts  for some of the equipment i've seen a  lot of photos of the of clinics in rural  parts of the country and they look  extremely dire no stockpile of equipment  really poor  equipment and supplies  very dirty not sanitary that could be  common in in some certain rural areas in  bigger cities  there are of course hospitals but  i just don't think that they're up to  international standards another thing to  remember is that this virus is sweeping  through a population where millions of  people are malnourished 42 according to  the u.n north korea has always struggled  to feed its people and over the last two  years there have been extra problems  north korea depends on imports from  china for many staple foods but when  they shut the border to keep out kovid  it also meant a lot of that food wasn't  coming in  a drought in 2020 and floods the  following year hurt farming and hit  production and when you've got a  population that's not well fed they're  going to be more vulnerable to disease  we know for instance in case of the  tuberculosis uh  disease  that north korea is one of the countries  with the highest burdens of that and  that speaks to the  sort of overall weak immune system of  the north korean population so how is  north korea tackling its covet outbreak  well the government launched what it's  calling a maximum emergency epidemic  prevention system it includes a national  lockdown with shops restaurants markets  and schools reportedly closed  but as always it's hard to know exactly  what the picture is and the differences  between the cities and the countryside i  haven't seen any evidence that police  are setting up walls or um boarding up  doors of buildings like we've seen in  in shanghai recently for example but  we've seen  blocked off streets a lot of shots of  empty streets it's really unclear at  this point but  um it seems to be that there's  hmi lockdown  regional lockdown but then potentially  exceptions state media has released  video of a factory that's still running  and grocery stores and pharmacies that  are open the army has been drafted in to  deliver medical supplies there's also a  public information campaign on things  like social distancing and washing hands  and recommending gargling with salt  water  [Music]  foreign  now when it comes to outside help there  have been plenty of offers including  from south korea the us and the who  but none have been accepted we've  offered vaccines we've got no response  we have offered support  in the form of technical assistance in  the form of medical supplies  tests vaccines  and we are  ready to deploy those the problem is  less about the  world's openness and  you know the readiness to help the north  korean population it has more to do with  the regimes  just being completely closed  to any form of outside help that is  going to  one expose the the vulnerabilities of  the country  and also too  it's also going to reflect poorly upon  kim's own competence as a leader but  north korea may have already turned to  its closest friend china for support  it looks like three cargo planes from  the state airline recently made a trip  to china they're thought to be the  airline's first flights out of the  country in the last two years  reports from south korean media suggest  they could have been picking up medical  supplies  ten days after north korea announced its  first coveted cases state media reported  that they're successfully overcoming the  crisis that the government's response  was working the official tally showed  the number of new cases coming down and  a total of just 66 deaths  but again this is north korea we're  talking about and its track record  suggests we need to be extremely  cautious about trusting the leadership  to be transparent when dealing with a  crisis  in case you missed it take a look at our  last episode on the tragic killing of al  jazeera's journalist shadeen abu akkle  [Music]

